TI  - Successful management of subglottic stenosis during the third trimester of 
      pregnancy.
PG  - 342-4
LID - 10.1097/LBR.0b013e3181f42da1 [doi]
AB  - Subglottic stenosis is seldom encountered during pregnancy. Definitive treatment 
      is tracheal resection and reanastomosis, although not during the later stages of 
      pregnancy. We describe 2 patients who presented during the third trimester of 
      pregnancy with significant tracheal obstruction from subglottic stenosis, one 
      caused by Wegener granulomatosis and the other idiopathic in nature. Both 
      patients refused temporary tracheostomy. We describe the surgical and anesthetic 
      management of these patients subsequent to which they were both able to have 
      normal, full-term vaginal deliveries.
