VI  - 17
IP  - 1
DP  - 2010 Jan
TI  - An unusual case of persistent extrinsic bronchial compression.
PG  - 93-4
LID - 10.1097/LBR.0b013e3181cd6ff4 [doi]
AB  - Bronchomalacia is defined as diffuse or segmental bronchial weakness and easy 
      compressibility. We present a rare case of a 67-year-old man with chronic cough 
      as a consequence of focal bronchomalacia caused by persistent extrinsic 
      compression of the left lower lobe bronchus by the descending aorta and the left 
      lower lobe branch of the pulmonary artery. Focal bronchomalacia discovered during 
      adulthood is most often acquired and can be attributed to extrinsic compression 
      by abnormal blood vessels due to hypertension and lung transplantation or to 
      bronchial disease processes. The natural history of bronchomalacia is typically 
      progressive.
