TI  - Endobronchial Ultrasound-Radial Probe-Assisted Cryobiopsy for Peripheral Lung 
      Mass: A New Way for Better Yield?
PG  - 67-70
LID - 10.1097/LBR.0000000000000184 [doi]
AB  - Solitary pulmonary nodules pose a diagnostic challenge as traditional techniques 
      like bronchoscopic transbronchial biopsies or percutaneous transthoracic needle 
      biopsies suffer either from poor yield or unacceptable complications. The advent 
      of endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS)-radial probe (RP) has helped to guide the 
      operator to the lesion more accurately and thereby improve yield. Small biopsy 
      forceps or cytology brushes can be passed through the guide sheath of the 
      EBUS-RP, after removing the probe, to get specimens. However, a small specimen 
      may sometimes prove inadequate for special molecular studies or genetic analysis. 
      Recently, there have been feasibility reports of transbronchial cryobiopsies that 
      can be safely performed with larger biopsy specimens. We report a case of 
      solitary pulmonary nodules where EBUS-RP was used to guide a cryoprobe to get 
      large transbronchial cryobiopsies successfully.
