TI  - Ultrasound-guided Percutaneous Needle Aspiration by Pulmonologists: A Study of 
      Factors With Impact on Procedural Yield and Complications.
PG  - 204-8
LID - 10.1097/LBR.0000000000000175 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: The availability of portable ultrasound (US) guidance has led to 
      usage by subspecialists outside of radiology. Several subspecialties now perform 
      percutaneous fine-needle aspiration (PFNA) for lesions relevant to their 
      expertise. This is a study of US-guided PFNA performed by interventional 
      pulmonologists at a single institution. METHODS: We retrospectively reviewed 
      serial US-guided PFNAs performed at an institution with the goals of defining 
      yield, factors relating to yield, and complication rates. RESULTS: A total of 111 
      consecutive lesions were evaluated and 109 biopsied during the study period. 
      Diagnosis was established in 81% of cases (90/109), with a total complication 
      rate of 15% but only 1 case of a need for a higher level of care. Factors 
      associated with increased diagnostic yield were suspicion of malignancy, 
      increased size, and pleural adhesions (lack of pleural sliding). The presence of 
      pleural sliding was associated with increased pneumothorax and decreased yield. 
      CONCLUSIONS: US-guided PFNA is a valuable tool for the interventional 
      pulmonologist. Both size and pleural sliding have an impact on diagnostic yield.
